WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.301
-Our next guest is a legendary

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:04.437
Emmy-winning television writer
and producer behind shows

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.438
like "All in the Family,"
"The Jeffersons,"

00:00:06.438 --> 00:00:07.707
and "Sanford and Son."

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:08.941 line:0%
His book, "All in the Family:

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:10.443 line:0%
The Show That Changed
Television,"

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.711 line:0%
is out November 2nd.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:14.480
Please welcome to the show
Norman Lear, everyone.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.516
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:19.118
-There he is!

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:21.420
-Norman,
I'm so happy to see you.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.457
You are 99, which means
you're in your 100th year,

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:27.160
and I'm assuming you get
asked this all the time.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.629
You're probably exhausted
answering it.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:31.631
But how do you do it, Norman?

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:34.033
[ Laughter ]

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.969
-Uh, I wake up every morning.

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.504
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:40.073
-That sounds --
That seems like that's probably

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.475
the key to the whole thing.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:44.978
-I always think about
two little words

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:47.880
we don't pay
enough attention to.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.682
"Over" and "next."

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.550
When something is over,
it's over,

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.619
and we are all on to next.

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:56.689
And if there was a hammock
in the middle,

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:58.324
that would be
the best definition

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:00.993
I could give
of living in the moment.

00:01:00.993 --> 00:01:05.331
So I think that's what
I have been able to do.

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:08.568
The fact that my life is --

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:11.170
It's taken me
every split second of my life

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.273
to finish this sentence.

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:15.141
[ Laughter ]

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.343
And it just took you every split
second of your life to go...

00:01:18.343 --> 00:01:19.612
[ Imitates laughter ]

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:21.881
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.650
So it's an important moment.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.452
-It was lovely
to read about this show.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:30.456
It must be nice going back and
revisiting it in your memory.

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:33.692
I want to ask -- You know, now,
I think, people would not

00:01:33.692 --> 00:01:36.329
even begin to think that
the show would not have worked,

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:39.264
but, you know, no show
is guaranteed success.

00:01:39.264 --> 00:01:42.668
When did you know
it was working?

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:45.738
-Are you talking about --
-"All in the Family."

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:49.475
-"All in the Family."
Uh...

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.978
You know,
I made it three year--

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:55.315
I made it three times
before it went on the air.

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.850
Made it in New York twice
and once here

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.087
before it went on the air.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:06.291
Always with Carroll O'Connor
and Jean Stapleton,

00:02:06.291 --> 00:02:08.961
but the first two times
absent

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:12.131
the Rob Reiner
and Sally Struthers.

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:15.267
We made it
with two other young people.

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:18.471
And it was a blessing that
the network didn't pick it up

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.875
because by the time it came --

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:27.146
by the time
we found Rob and Sally,

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:30.650
the chemistry
between each of those four

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.951
in every direction

00:02:32.951 --> 00:02:36.789
was so glorious and so --

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:42.328
That was, you know,
the fate of the gods.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:45.097
-It was not universally loved
by critics

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:46.733
when it first came out.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:49.636
You would respond to the critics
when this happened.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:51.470
Do you remember
any of that back-and-forth

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.572
or do you remember
why people didn't like it

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:55.608
right out of the gate?

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:58.744
-Uh, well, they didn't like --

00:02:58.744 --> 00:03:06.719
You know, didn't understand
the reason for a bigot...

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.687
as the center of a show.

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:12.858
And there was a review

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:18.464
by the name of a very
well-known author and critic.

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:22.267
A woman. I can't remember
her name for the moment.

00:03:22.267 --> 00:03:28.340
Anyway, she gave it a terrible
review in "The New York Times,"

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:33.178
and then about six weeks later,
she wrote another review

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:35.848
and took back what she had said
the first time

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:38.484
because she understood
the show better.

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:40.419
-That's really amazing.
That doesn't happen.

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:43.289
How many times have you had
a critic change their mind

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:45.224
in your 100 years?

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:47.527
-That was it.
-That was it, yeah.

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:52.498
-My soon-to-be 100 years.
I'm only working on 100.

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:54.634
-Did you --

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.836
I've heard
you describe yourself as --

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:59.238
I've always thought of myself
as a writer

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:01.307
who works well under deadlines
because, you know,

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.742
I think like a lot of writers,
I procrastinate.

00:04:03.742 --> 00:04:05.644
But I've heard you
call yourself --

00:04:05.644 --> 00:04:07.679
I like this term a lot more
than "procrastinator."

00:04:07.679 --> 00:04:11.384
You're a "clutch writer."
Is that what I think it means?

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:14.119
-[ Laughs ]
Well, I spent the --

00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:18.357
many years
at the beginning of my career.

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:21.627
I had to deliver,
let's say on a Monday morning,

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:23.763
a monologue

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:28.434
for Tennessee Ernie Ford
or George Gobel,

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:32.839
and I'd start on it
the Wednesday preceding.

00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:36.742
I'd be caught in a clutch.
[ Laughs ]

00:04:36.742 --> 00:04:39.711
Wednesday, Thursday,
Friday, Saturday,

00:04:39.711 --> 00:04:42.848
and sometimes Sunday.

00:04:42.848 --> 00:04:47.086
Many times late at night.
Writing all night.

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:51.057
And finishing
what I was responsible for

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:53.759
five days earlier.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:56.929
And without any sleep,

00:04:56.929 --> 00:05:00.632
going down to Hollywood

00:05:00.632 --> 00:05:06.772
to a reading
of what I had just written.

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:09.641
It was scary as hell,

00:05:09.641 --> 00:05:13.012
uh, but we got through it.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:15.948
-You received viewer mail
over the years, as well.

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:19.285
Not just about "All in the
Family," but all of your shows.

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:23.623
Was there a piece of viewer mail
you remember with any fondness?

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:26.058
-Uh...

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:31.330
I remember a piece of mail
where it called me

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.233
the number-one enemy
of the American family

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:36.769
in our generation.

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:40.540
And I didn't know his name
at first,

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.308
and then later I learned --

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:44.543
Wait a minute.
The guy who wrote it

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:47.546
is on the air,
and he's a reverend.

00:05:47.546 --> 00:05:49.448
It's Jerry Falwell.

00:05:49.448 --> 00:05:50.816
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.816 --> 00:05:53.185
Called me the number-one enemy
of the American family.

00:05:53.185 --> 00:05:56.122
And then I heard
from Pat Robertson,

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:59.125
who certified
that Falwell was correct.

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:00.760
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.428
-Well, if it was just
one of them who said it,

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:05.164
I would take your side,
but if it was both of them...

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:07.967
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:10.135
You --
I guess at a young age --

00:06:10.135 --> 00:06:13.406
Someone who chooses his career
and sticks with it for so long,

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:15.374
at a young age,
you must have known

00:06:15.374 --> 00:06:17.543
you had not just a good
sense of humor,

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:20.379
but maybe the ability to see
humor where others weren't.

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:23.749
Was that the case?

00:06:23.749 --> 00:06:28.020
-You know,
when I was 9 years old,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:32.258
my father got into trouble
and was sent to prison.

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:36.062
Something to do with fraud.

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:40.166
And the night he was --

00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:44.103
Two nights
after he was called away,

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:46.939
my mother had to give up

00:06:46.939 --> 00:06:50.910
the apartment we were living in
and so forth.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.644
She was selling the furniture.

00:06:52.644 --> 00:06:57.116
I was going off to live
with my grandparents.

00:06:57.116 --> 00:07:00.419
Anyway, there were strangers
in the house looking at --

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.220
The thing that killed me most

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.323
was my father's
red leather chair

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:07.860
where we used to sit together
to listen --

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.862
I had radio.
No television then.

00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:13.466
To listen to the fights
on Friday night

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:18.104
from Madison Square Garden.

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:19.371
Very sentimental to me.

00:07:19.371 --> 00:07:23.042
And I was in --
not in good shape.

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:28.347
When a stranger who was
looking at my father's chair,

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:32.384
you know, about to purchase it.

00:07:32.384 --> 00:07:34.219
Put his hand -- [ Laughs ]

00:07:34.219 --> 00:07:35.988
Put his hand on my shoulder
and said,

00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:40.592
"Well, Norman, you're
the man of the house now."

00:07:40.592 --> 00:07:44.163
And somehow,
at 9 years of age,

00:07:44.163 --> 00:07:47.600
I knew that was hilarious.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.001
[ Laughter ]

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:51.270
I wanted to say
"[bleep] hilarious,"

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:55.207
but I don't know
whether I can do that or not.

00:07:55.207 --> 00:07:57.976
-Well, Norman,

00:07:57.976 --> 00:08:00.312
it was an honor
to speak with you,

00:08:00.312 --> 00:08:02.682
but even more important
than that,

00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:04.517
it was an absolute delight.

00:08:04.517 --> 00:08:06.618
Thank you for making time
for us tonight.

00:08:06.618 --> 00:08:08.454
-I couldn't have
enjoyed it more,

00:08:08.454 --> 00:08:10.956
and I think the world of you,
Seth Meyers.

00:08:10.956 --> 00:08:13.859
-[ Audience awws ]
-Thank you, Norman Lear.

00:08:13.859 --> 00:08:16.662 line:0%
"All in the Family: The Show
That Changed Television"

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:18.364 line:0%
is out November 2nd.

